==English==
{{was wotd|2025|June|2}}

===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|dʰéǵʰōm}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|omertà}}; further etymology uncertain—the following have been suggested:<ref name="OED"/>{{R:OED online|pos=n|id=8127474876|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

* From {{der|en|es|hombredad|t=manliness}} {{qualifier|archaic}}, with the spelling aligned with {{der|en|scn|omu|t=man}}. {{m|es||Hombredad}} is derived from {{m|es|hombre|t=man}} + {{m|es|-edad}} (variant of {{m|es|-dad|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|noun}}s denoting a state of being}}). (However, {{m|scn|*ummirità}} would be the natural phonological output.)
* From {{der|en|scn|umirtà|t=humility}} (referring to the Mafia code’s requirement of obedience to the leader), from {{der|en|it|umiltà|t=humility}}, from {{der|en|la|humilitās|t=obedience, submission}}, from {{m|la|humilis|t=humble; abject, submissive}} + {{m|la|-tās|pos=suffix forming {{glossary|feminine}} {{glossary|abstract noun}}s denoting a state of being}}; with {{m|la||humilis}} from {{m|la|humus|t=floor, ground; earth, soil}} + {{m|la|-ilis|pos=suffix forming an {{glossary|adjective}} of relation}}. If so, the English word is a {{doublet|en|humility|nocap=1}}. However, the ''{{w|Oxford English Dictionary}}'' takes the view that this is “not well supported by the geographical distribution of the word”.

